🗂️ Recommended folder structure in the new project
To avoid changing more code than necessary, the easiest approach is to keep the same expected structure inside the new Katalon project:

• Groovy classes under the package jiraIntegration
• Jira request objects under Object Repository/Jira Integration/
• report output under the project Reports folder
• logo asset under Image/gaia-logo.png
• local Test Plan key cache under TestPlanKeys/
• optional LastRun.xlsx in the project root if your flow uses it

Keeping this structure reduces the number of code changes required and makes the migration faster and safer.



🚀 Minimum setup steps in the new project
To make the integration work in another project, the recommended setup order is:

Copy the integration classes
Copy or recreate the Jira request objects
Copy the report logo if the HTML report will still use it
Confirm the new project generates a supported XML result file
Update the configuration values inside JiraFlowCoordinator
Review Jira request object URLs and payloads
Test the flow with one suite only
Confirm that the Test Plan is created or reused correctly
Confirm that the Test Execution is created and updated
Confirm that gaiaReport.html, Results.zip, and Evidences.zip are uploaded correctly


⚙️ Main file to configure first
The first file that must be updated in the new project is:

• JiraFlowCoordinator

This class contains the main integration configuration and controls the overall publishing flow. It is the central place where the new project identity, Jira mapping, naming, and file conventions should be adjusted first.



🔑 Variables that must be updated for the new project
Inside JiraFlowCoordinator, review and update at least the following values:

Jira connection and issue mapping

• JIRA_TOKEN
• PROJECT_KEY
• EPIC_KEY
• JIRA_BASE_URL

These values are project-specific and must match the Jira instance and Jira project used by the new automation repository.

Execution metadata

• ENVIRONMENT
• LABELS

These should reflect the target environment and the labeling standard used by the new project.

Optional behavior flags

• MANUAL_TEST_PLAN_KEY
• FORCE_CREATE_NEW_TEST_PLAN
• RESET_SAVED_TEST_PLAN_KEY

These values control how Test Plans are reused or recreated and may stay blank or false initially unless the new project needs a fixed behavior.

Naming and file conventions

• TEST_PLAN_SUFFIX
• PRIMARY_XML_FILE_NAME
• SECONDARY_XML_FILE_NAME
• GAIA_REPORT_FILE_NAME
• RESULT_ZIP_NAME
• EVIDENCE_FOLDER_NAME
• EVIDENCE_ZIP_NAME

These must match the file outputs generated by the new project. If the new project produces a different XML file name or uses another evidence folder name, these values must be updated here.



🧪 What the new project must generate for this integration to work
The integration expects that the project execution produces at least one valid XML result file. By default, the current code looks for:

• cucumber.xml
• JUnit_Report.xml

If the new project generates a different XML file name, update:

• PRIMARY_XML_FILE_NAME
• SECONDARY_XML_FILE_NAME

The integration also expects the report folder to contain the execution artifacts and optionally an evidence folder with screenshots or images. If the new project uses another folder name for evidence, change:

• EVIDENCE_FOLDER_NAME



🧾 What must be updated in the Jira request objects
Even if the classes are copied correctly, the integration will not work in a new project unless the Jira request objects are reviewed and aligned with the new Jira setup.

Review these points in every used request object

• Jira base URL
• Bearer token variable mapping
• Project key variable mapping
• Epic field mapping
• Request body field names
• Endpoint version
• Object name and folder path consistency



🖼️ If the new project is not equal in layout?

If the new project is not the same identity like GAIA, you should also review the visual naming in the custom HTML report builder.

Files to review

• JiraGaiaReportBuilder
• JiraGaiaHtmlTemplateBuilder



Most common updates

• Report title prefix
• Application name shown in the banner
• Report subtitle text
• Logo file path
• Logo image file
• Evidence labels if needed



For example, in JiraGaiaHtmlTemplateBuilder, review constants such as:

• APP_NAME
• REPORT_TITLE_PREFIX
• LOGO_SRC

If you want the report to show another application name, branding, or logo, change them there.



🏷️ Naming rules that should be reviewed in the new project

The current integration builds names automatically from the suite name. This is useful, but it also means the naming logic may need adjustment depending on how the new project organizes its suites.

Review these methods in JiraFlowCoordinator

• getCurrentSuiteName()
• getExecutionSummary()
• getTestPlanName()



What to validate

• How the suite name is extracted from the report folder
• Whether timestamp folders are used the same way
• Whether the Test Plan naming rule fits the new project
• Whether the execution summary format is acceptable in Jira



If the new project has different suite naming conventions, these methods are likely the first place to adjust.



💾 Saved Test Plan key reuse in the new project

The integration stores resolved Test Plan keys locally by suite name under the TestPlanKeys folder. This helps reuse Test Plans between executions and avoids searching Jira every time.

For a first migration test, It is recommended to start with:

• RESET_SAVED_TEST_PLAN_KEY = true

This forces the new project to ignore any old cached mapping and resolve the correct Test Plan fresh during the first test run. After the first successful run, this can be changed back if you want the local reuse behavior.

📎 Attachments and ZIP packaging in the new project



The integration packages and uploads:

• gaiaReport.html
• Evidences.zip
• Results.zip



If the new project has a different artifact structure, review:

• JiraExecutionPublisher.shouldSkipReportEntry()
• JiraExecutionPublisher.shouldSkipEvidenceEntry()
• JiraExecutionPublisher.zipReports()
• JiraExecutionPublisher.zipEvidences()

These methods define what is included or excluded from the uploaded ZIP files. If the new project stores useful artifacts in other folders, or if some folders should no longer be skipped, update the exclusion rules there.



🖼️ Evidence and screenshot behavior in the new project

If the new project stores screenshots in a different way, review the evidence discovery logic.

Main files to review

• JiraFlowCoordinator
• JiraGaiaReportBuilder

Main points to validate

• Evidence folder name
• Evidence search roots
• Screenshot file extensions
• Skip rules for unwanted images
• How failed screenshots are matched to scenarios



If the new project uses another evidence naming pattern or folder structure, you will likely need to adapt:

• getEvidenceSearchRoots()
• collectFailureImages()
• attachEvidenceToFailedScenarios()



🧩 What to change if the package name changes

If you do not want to keep the package jiraIntegration, you can rename it, but then all affected classes must be updated consistently. The safest option is to keep the original package name during the first migration. Renaming the package is optional and should only be done after the integration is already working in the new project.



🚨 Common reasons why the migration fails
The most common causes of failure when reusing this integration in another project are:

• wrong Jira token
• wrong Jira base URL
• wrong project key
• wrong Epic field ID
• XML file not found because the new project uses another name
• evidence folder not matching the configured folder name
• request object URL still pointing to old Jira environment
• execution created but not linked to Test Plan due to Xray endpoint mismatch
• execution metadata update failing because the Jira field does not exist in the new project
• attachment upload failing because the file path is wrong or the file is empty
• report branding still pointing to old GAIA logo or old application name

